<h2 id="glory-to-god-for-all-things">Glory to God for All Things</h2>

<p>Outside the town of Comana in Pontus, five or six miles beyond the wall, stood the shrine of a martyr named Basiliscus. In September 407 a small escort of soldiers brought a sick prisoner back to it. They had set out from the shrine that morning and covered about thirty furlongs before he became so ill that they had to turn around. He had been hurried along the roads for three months, through hot sun and heavy rain, and he could go no farther.</p>

<p>When they reached the shrine again, the prisoner asked for white clothes. He took off everything he was wearing, down to his shoes, and put the white garments on. What he had taken off he gave away to the people standing around him. Then he received Communion and prayed one last time, in the words he always used: "Glory to God for all things."</p>

<p>Three years earlier this man had been the archbishop of Constantinople, the emperor's own city. Crowds had left the horse races and the theater to hear him preach, and pressed around him so hard that he had to sit in the middle of them to be heard. A fallen consul had once lain beneath the altar of his church, hiding for his life, while he preached over him. Now a synod of bishops had deposed him, the court had sent him to the far edge of the empire, and his guards were driving him on at forced speed toward the Black Sea.</p>

<p>Later generations gave him the name by which the world knows him: Chrysostom, the golden-mouthed, for the power of his preaching. The Orthodox Church honors him as one of the Three Hierarchs, beside Basil the Great and Gregory the Theologian, and the Divine Liturgy celebrated in Orthodox and Byzantine Catholic churches bears his name. The Catholic Church counts him among its Doctors. He told consuls and empresses the truth to their faces, and he taught that a poor man lying in the market place is an altar too, made of the very members of Christ.</p>

<p>Yet as a young man John had been afraid of exactly the life he ended up living. He believed that the office of bishop was where souls were lost, and he once hid from it so well that his dearest friend was seized in his place. He knew his own temper, and so did those who knew him. How did that man, carried by force into the most exposed see in the empire and then stripped of everything, come to die in white, giving thanks? The answer is the whole of his life, and it begins in Antioch, in a widow's house.</p>

<h2 id="a-widows-bedroom-in-antioch">A Widow's Bedroom in Antioch</h2>

<p>John was born in Antioch, in Syria, in the middle of the fourth century. His father, Secundus, held the office of military commander in Syria, and his mother was named Anthusa. There was one other child, an elder sister. His father died soon after John was born, and Anthusa did not marry again. She kept the boy's inheritance whole and spent her own dowry on his education.</p>

<p>The education was the best that Antioch could give. John studied rhetoric under Libanius and philosophy under Andragathius, and he was meant for the law courts. Decades later the historian Sozomen told a story about the old teacher on his deathbed. Asked who should take his place, Libanius is said to have answered: "It would have been John, replied he, had not the Christians taken him from us."</p>

<p>The Christians did take him, and early. At eighteen, his friend and biographer Palladius writes, John "revolted against the professors of verbosities" and turned to the Scriptures. Meletius, the bishop of Antioch, noticed the bright young man and kept him near. In his early twenties John was baptized, and after three years in the bishop's company he became a reader. Then he went further. With two fellow students from Libanius's school, Theodore and Maximus, he joined a small community of young men who lived a strict ascetic life under the teachers Diodorus and Carterius. When Theodore wavered, planning to marry and go back to the life of the city, John wrote him a letter that called him back, and Theodore repented and gave up his property. Both friends later became bishops: Theodore of Mopsuestia and Maximus of Seleucia.</p>

<p>He was not an easy young man. One friend of those years remembered his "proneness to irritability, rather than to modesty." But there was another friend whom he loved above all the rest. Years later he began his most famous early book by describing him: of all his "genuine and true friends," he wrote, there was one "who excelled all the rest in his attachment to me."</p>

<p>He calls this friend Basil. Basil wanted the two of them to leave their homes and share one dwelling, living together for God, and John agreed. Then Anthusa found out.</p>

<p>She took her son into her own room and sat beside him on the bed where she had given birth to him, and she wept. His father, she reminded him, had died soon after that birth. She had kept his inheritance and spent her dowry on him, and she had never married again. She asked only one thing in return.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>"Do not plunge me into a second widowhood; nor revive the grief which is now laid to rest: wait for my death."</p>
  <p><cite>— Anthusa to her son, in John Chrysostom, On the Priesthood, Book I, 5</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>John stayed. The shared house with Basil never came to be. But the two friends were about to face a harder test than any a mother could set, because the Church of Antioch had its eye on both of them.</p>

<h2 id="the-friend-who-was-caught">The Friend Who Was Caught</h2>

<p>In those days a man could be seized for the office of bishop whether he wanted it or not, as both friends were about to learn. John had promised Basil that whatever happened, they would act together. When the time came, John hid.</p>

<p>Basil, who knew nothing of the plan, was seized. When he protested, some of the men standing by told him how strange it was that John, the more hot-tempered of the two, "had yielded very mildly to the judgment of the Fathers," while Basil was making a scene. Believing that his friend had gone first, Basil gave way. Then he learned the truth, and he came to John hurt and reproachful.</p>

<p>John tells this story himself, in his dialogue <em>On the Priesthood</em>, a book written as a long conversation with the friend he had tricked. In it Basil accuses him, and John defends himself, until at last he stops defending and confesses. "I will now try and unveil to you the storm of my soul," he says. He had not hidden because he thought little of the office. He had hidden because he was afraid of what it would do to him.</p>

<p>Of all the rocks on which a priest's soul could be wrecked, he wrote, the worst was vainglory, the hunger for praise. On that rock lived wild beasts, and they would tear him apart if he were given charge of a church.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>"Do you ask what those wild beasts are? They are wrath, despondency, envy, strife, slanders, accusations, falsehood, hypocrisy, intrigues … love of praise, desire of honor (which indeed most of all drives the human soul headlong to perdition) … contempt of the poor, paying court to the rich."</p>
  <p><cite>— John Chrysostom, On the Priesthood, Book III, 9</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>He never lost that fear. In a sermon on the Acts of the Apostles, speaking to men who wanted the office, he put it more bluntly still. "I do not think there are many among Bishops that will be saved, but many more that perish: and the reason is, that it is an affair that requires a great mind." Nearly every beast on his list would one day come for him, in a city he had not yet seen. Some would come from his enemies. Some, his critics said, came from his own nature. But that was still far ahead. For now, having escaped the bishop's throne, he left the city altogether.</p>

<h2 id="six-years-on-the-mountain">Six Years on the Mountain</h2>

<p>He went up into the mountains, and for four years he lived under the direction of an old man named Syrus. Then he went further, into a cave, alone. He stayed there two years, and for the greater part of that time, Palladius writes, he denied himself sleep. He would not lie down. The cold of the mountain and the sleepless nights ruined his stomach and his kidneys, and in the end it was illness that brought him back down to the Church in the city.</p>

<p>He paid for those six years for the rest of his life. He could not drink wine, which sent the blood to his head. His stomach was disordered, and when he was absorbed in the Scriptures he forgot to eat at all. Long afterward, when he was a bishop and would not dine with anyone, his enemies called it pride. Palladius, who knew him, said it was his stomach.</p>

<p>Back in Antioch he took up his service at the altar again, and after five years of it Meletius ordained him a deacon. As a deacon he wrote the dialogue on the priesthood, a treatise against Stagirius, a treatise on the incomprehensibility of God, and a book on the women who lived in the houses of clergy. Then Bishop Flavian ordained him a priest, and for twelve years he served as a priest in Antioch.</p>

<p>The young man who had hidden from the altar now stood beside it and preached. His great test came in one Lent, when the whole city waited to learn what the emperor would do to it.</p>

<h2 id="the-spring-the-statues-fell">The Spring the Statues Fell</h2>

<p>Early in the year 387, John preached on a verse from Paul's first letter to Timothy: "Use a little wine for your stomach's sake." It was a sermon about moderation. He had no way of knowing that it was the last calm sermon Antioch would hear for weeks.</p>

<p>A new imperial tax had been announced, and the city's anger boiled over. A crowd pulled down the statues of the emperor Theodosius, and a bronze statue of the empress Placilla was dragged down with them. Then the anger turned to terror. Word came that the emperor might reduce Antioch to the rank of a village. The baths were closed and members of the city council were thrown into prison, and people began to vanish from the forum, seized and dragged before the judges. When John next stood up to preach, he described the emptied city.</p>

<p>Before, he said, the people of Antioch had buzzed about the forum every day like bees around their hive. "But behold now, this hive has become solitary! For even as smoke does those bees, so fear has driven away our swarms." Free men sat shut indoors. In the forum there was "hardly perchance one or two people, and those hanging their heads and creeping about." It was, he said, "a flight without enemies; an expulsion of inhabitants without a battle; a captivity without capture!" He had opened that sermon by refusing to make a speech at all.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>"The present season is one for tears, and not for words; for lamentation, not for discourse; for prayer, not for preaching."</p>
  <p><cite>— John Chrysostom, Homilies on the Statues, Homily II, 1</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>The city's one hope was its bishop. Flavian was an old man, at the far edge of old age, and his health was poor. It was a hard season for travel, the great feast was near, and his only sister lay dying. He left all of it and set out for Constantinople to beg the emperor for mercy. John preached the next sermon beside the empty bishop's chair. "When I look on that throne, deserted and bereft of our teacher," he told the people, "I rejoice and weep at the same time."</p>

<p>Then he went to see the trials for himself. A crowd stood outside the doors of the court, and yet it was as silent, he said, "as though there had been no man there"; no one dared ask his neighbor anything. Inside stood soldiers with swords and clubs. At the very entrance a prisoner's mother and sister were rolling on the pavement, within hearing of "the voice of the executioners, the strokes of the scourge, the wailing of those who were being scourged." At evening the condemned were loaded with chains and led through the forum to prison.</p>

<p>Into that silence came the monks. Unasked, the hermits of the mountains left their caves and huts "as if they had been so many angels arriving from heaven," John said, and they sat all day before the court, ready to snatch the condemned from the executioners. They told the judges they would not leave until the city was spared, or until they were sent to the emperor with the accused, and they were ready to die with them. John saw the mother of one of the accused uncover her grey hair, take the judge's horse by the bridle, and run beside it through the forum into the court. The city's philosophers, he pointed out, had run off and hidden in caves. The men of the desert had come into the city. "Our city has become all at once a monastery," he said.</p>

<p>Through those weeks John kept returning to one phrase: "Blessed be God." Day after day through that Lent he preached to the frightened city, twenty-one sermons in all. Then a fast horseman rode in ahead of the bishop with the news. The emperor had forgiven Antioch. Even the men in chains, John told his people, had refused to be frightened by rumors while Flavian was away: "We have fixed our safety upon the sacred anchor!" On Easter Day he blessed God for restoring "the head to the body, the shepherd to the sheep," while outside the people hung garlands in the forum, lit lamps, and spread green leaves before the shops.</p>

<p>Antioch never forgot the priest who had stood with it that spring. Ten years later its love for him would be the very reason he had to be stolen from it.</p>

<h2 id="a-spiritual-school-not-a-theater">A Spiritual School, Not a Theater</h2>

<p>For twelve years John preached in Antioch, often more than once a week and sometimes several days running. People came in crowds, and when he pleased them, they applauded. He would not have it. "Did ye give praise to what has been said? Nay, I want not applause, nor tumults, nor noise," he told them in one sermon. "One thing only do I wish, that quietly and intelligently listening, you should do what is said." He reminded them that they were not "gazing on actors." "This place is a spiritual school."</p>

<p>His rivals were the theater and the racecourse, and he knew it. "I see you spending your day in horse races, and theatres," he told his hearers, and many of them ran straight from his sermons to the circus. He warned them that he would keep at it: "I will make the knife sharper, and the cut deeper; and I will not cease, till I have scattered the theatre of the devil, and so purified the assembly of the Church." He did not tell them to go up to the mountains; they could stay with house and wife and children. But he grieved that in the apostles' time "the houses were churches, but now the church has become a house."</p>

<p>Above all he refused to let them honor Christ in gold and ignore Him in rags. In one sermon on Matthew he set the gold of the altar beside the poor outside the church.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>"Would you do honor to Christ's body? Neglect Him not when naked; do not while here you honor Him with silken garments, neglect Him perishing without of cold and nakedness."</p>
  <p><cite>— John Chrysostom, Homilies on Matthew, Homily 50, 4</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>"God has no need at all of golden vessels," he said in the same sermon, "but of golden souls." In another he told his people that the poor man was himself an altar. "This altar is composed of the very members of Christ," he said. "You honor indeed this altar, because it receives Christ's body; but him that is himself the body of Christ you treat with contumely, and when perishing, neglectest. This altar may thou everywhere see lying, both in lanes and in market places."</p>

<p>There is a harder side to these years, and it should be told plainly. In 386 and 387 John preached eight sermons aimed at Christians of Antioch who were keeping Jewish feasts and fasts. Their editors have titled them as sermons against those who were "Judaizing." But his fury fell on the Jews themselves. He called the synagogue a place worse than a brothel and a dwelling of demons, and he held the Jews responsible for the death of Christ. Some scholars read these sermons as an example of a set form of ancient rhetorical blame. Others count them among the most violent attacks on Jews in all of Christian writing. They are part of his story, and no account of his preaching is honest without them.</p>

<p>The same voice that could wound could also hold a frightened city together. In the autumn of 397 the archbishop of Constantinople died, and the court began to look for a preacher.</p>

<h2 id="kidnapped-at-the-romanesia-gate">Kidnapped at the Romanesia Gate</h2>

<p>On 27 September 397, Nectarius, the archbishop of Constantinople, died. The court wanted John to succeed him. The idea came from the court eunuch Eutropius, who had come to know John when the emperor's business took him to the East. There was one difficulty. Antioch would not give up its preacher quietly, and the court feared a riot.</p>

<p>So the thing was done in secret. The governor of Antioch sent for John to meet him at the shrines of the martyrs outside the city, near the gate called Romanesia. When John arrived, he was put into a public carriage and handed over to a eunuch sent by Eutropius and a guard. The historian Sozomen tells it a little differently: Asterius, the general commanding in the East, had John get into his own chariot, drove him at speed to the military station of Pagras, and there handed him over to imperial officers. The two accounts agree on what matters. John was taken out of Antioch without a farewell, so that the city could not stop it.</p>

<p>The young man who had once hidden from a bishop's chair was now being carried, with no chance to hide, to the most exposed throne in the Church of the East.</p>

<p>In Constantinople another man was unhappy. Theophilus, the patriarch of Alexandria, was to consecrate the new archbishop, and he had a candidate of his own, a priest of his named Isidore. Eutropius settled the matter. There were charges against Theophilus that could be brought to trial, and Eutropius gave him a choice: he could consecrate John, or he could face the charges. Theophilus consecrated John on 26 February 398. He would not forget how it had been done.</p>

<h2 id="a-bishop-who-dined-alone">A Bishop Who Dined Alone</h2>

<p>The new archbishop went through his household and his clergy like a man cleaning a neglected house. Palladius lists what he did. John attacked the custom, called a "brotherly life," by which men in the Church shared their homes with women. He had the accounts of the Church's treasurer examined. He looked at what the bishop's household spent on itself and sent the money to the hospital instead. Then he built more hospitals, each with two priests, doctors, and cooks to care for the sick. He reviewed the list of widows supported by the Church, started night vigils of prayer, and watched the people who had spent their evenings at the horse races and the theater begin to come to church instead.</p>

<p>They came in such numbers that the crowd became dangerous. People shoved and pressed to get near enough to hear him, until John came down among them, took a seat on the readers' platform, and taught the crowd from there. When the Arians of the city marched through the streets at dawn singing hymns against the Trinity, he answered with night processions of his own, with silver crosses and lighted wax tapers carried before the singers. The Arians threw stones, there was blood on both sides, and the empress's own eunuch Briso, who had been put in charge of the processions, was struck on the forehead. The emperor stopped the Arian marches.</p>

<p>His clergy did not enjoy their new archbishop. Socrates says John treated them with more severity than was proper. His deacon Serapion made it worse with one remark, made in front of them all: "You will never be able to govern these men, my lord, unless you drive them all with a rod."</p>

<p>Then there was the matter of dinner. John ate alone. His friends gave three reasons: his ruined stomach, his years in the cave, and the poor. The clergy, Palladius explained, would have been content with nothing less than lavish hospitality, "punctually to the minute," and "it would have been absurd to waste the food of the sick or the poor on feasting the healthy." Everyone wanted to dine with him, Palladius admitted, "either to get a blessing, or because he was poor, or because he was greedy." His enemies saw a proud man who thought himself too good for their tables. Sozomen collected what people called him: hard, passionate, morose, arrogant.</p>

<p>Palladius saw another side. With his own disciples and clergy John liked to tease, and if he caught one of them boasting of his self-discipline, he gave him a nickname meaning the opposite. He would call "the teetotaller a drunkard," Palladius remembered, and "the charitable man a thief." The same Sozomen who listed his faults also wrote that John fought every injustice "as if they had been perpetrated against himself." And the severe archbishop was a merciful preacher. To sinners afraid that they had used up God's patience, he said: "Approach, although you may have repented a thousand times." Even some of his friends thought this went too far, and Sisinnius, the bishop of the Novatians, wrote a whole book against that one sentence.</p>

<p>Among those who came to him was a widow named Olympias. She had lost her husband before they had been married twenty months, and the emperor Theodosius had wanted her to marry his kinsman Elpidius. She refused him. "If my King had desired me to live with a husband," she said, "he would not have taken away my first." The emperor put her fortune in trust until she turned thirty. She told him he would do better still "if you order it to be dispersed among the poor, and the Churches." In the end he gave her property back.</p>

<p>Nectarius had made her a deaconess. Now she gave her wealth away so freely that John took her in hand. She had been "bestowing wealth on the wealthy," he told her, "which is as useless as if you had cast it into the sea." She ate no meat and seldom went to the baths, and she made sure her archbishop, with his bad stomach, had his daily bread. Palladius says John accepted her food a day at a time, "like a man ashamed of himself." Their friendship would outlast everything else he had in Constantinople.</p>

<p>Not all his zeal was gentle. Theodoret records that John sent monks, armed with imperial edicts and paid for by wealthy women, to destroy the pagan temples of Phoenicia. Whatever he did, he did with his whole force. Soon the man who had brought him to Constantinople would learn what that meant.</p>

<h2 id="a-consul-under-the-altar">A Consul Under the Altar</h2>

<p>Eutropius, the eunuch who had brought John to the capital, had been born a slave in Mesopotamia and passed from master to master, once as part of a bride's dowry. Now he rose higher still. He became a patrician and then a consul. He also passed a law that took away the right of sanctuary, the protection that a hunted man could claim by fleeing into a church.</p>

<p>Then he fell from power, and he ran to the only place left to him: the church whose protection his own law had removed. Soldiers came from the court to drag him out. He clung to the altar, and John saw his face "no better than the countenance of one dead," and heard "the chattering of his teeth." The soldiers' rage, John said later, "burned more fiercely than fire," and the archbishop himself was hurried away to the palace. He was not afraid. "I saw the swords and I meditated on Heaven," he said; "I expected death, and I bethought me of the resurrection."</p>

<p>The next day Eutropius lay under the altar table in front of the whole congregation, and the archbishop he had made went up to preach over him. He began with the words of Ecclesiastes: "'Vanity of vanities, all is vanity'—it is always seasonable to utter this but more especially at the present time." Then he turned to the man beneath the altar. "Where are now the brilliant surroundings of thy consulship? where are the gleaming torches?" "Was I not continually telling thee that wealth was a runaway?" He had spoken hard words to Eutropius before, as a friend does. "My wounds work health," he said, "but their kisses have produced an incurable disease."</p>

<p>Then he turned the sermon on its hinge. The theaters Eutropius had courted had betrayed him, but "the Church which you treated as an enemy has opened her bosom and received thee into it." Some of his hearers thought he was kicking a man when he was down, and some thought he should never have let Eutropius into the sanctuary at all. John answered both. He spoke, he said, "not as trampling upon one who is prostrate, but from a desire to make those who are still standing more secure."</p>

<p>The Church kept its refuge as long as Eutropius stayed in it. A few days later he left the church, and he was taken. "The Church hath no feet," John told his people; if Eutropius had not abandoned the Church, he would not have been surrendered. He was later beheaded at Chalcedon.</p>

<p>John faced a harder man soon after. Gainas, a general who had come into the empire from beyond the Danube, was an Arian, and he wanted one of the churches of the capital for his own people. The emperor Arcadius was ready to promise it. John refused, and Theodoret preserved his answer to the emperor.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>"Attempt, sir, no such promise, nor order what is holy to be given to the dogs."</p>
  <p><cite>— John Chrysostom to the emperor Arcadius, in Theodoret, Ecclesiastical History, Book V, 32</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>He told Gainas himself to remember what kind of clothes he had worn before he crossed the Danube into the empire. Later John went to Thrace as the emperor's envoy to the same general, and Theodoret tells what happened when they met. Gainas "placed his right hand upon his eyes," and brought his children to John's knees.</p>

<p>That was what a word from John could do. It was also why some powerful people had begun to fear him, and why the next quarrel, which began far from the capital, would be used against him.</p>

<h2 id="two-days-without-food">Two Days Without Food</h2>

<p>It began with a bishop who would not keep quiet. During a synod at Constantinople, Eusebius of Valentinopolis rose and accused Antoninus, the bishop of Ephesus, of seven crimes. Antoninus had melted down church plate, Eusebius said, taken the marble of the baptistery for his own bathroom, set up church pillars in his dining room, and sold the office of bishop for money. John tried to calm him. "I beg you not to bring a written charge against our brother Antoninus; we will set right the matters which have vexed you."</p>

<p>Eusebius would not be calmed. When John went into the church for the liturgy, Eusebius slipped in after him and, in front of the whole congregation, pressed a written accusation on him, conjuring him by terrible oaths, "by the salvation of the king and queen." The people thought he was begging for a reprieve from a death sentence. To quiet them John took the paper. Then he asked another bishop to offer the sacrifice and withdrew, Palladius explains, because "he always objected to offering the sacrifice with a disturbed mind."</p>

<p>The case dragged on. Antoninus died, and the Church of Ephesus begged John "with an awful adjuration" to come and set it in order. He was seriously ill, and it was the stormy season of winter. He sailed anyway. A storm from the north drove the ship to shelter under a mountain, and "for two whole days they lay without food," the ship rolling heavily at anchor. On the third day they landed, and John and his companions went the rest of the way to Ephesus on foot. There seventy bishops of Lydia, Asia, and Caria gathered. Six bishops who had bought their consecrations from Antoninus confessed it. They were removed from office, allowed to recover their money from his heirs, and replaced by six unmarried men. To succeed Antoninus John chose Heraclides, a Cypriot who had been his own deacon. He also took a number of churches from the Novatians and the Quartodecimans, and his enemies afterward called it violence.</p>

<p>Theophilus would later write that John had deposed sixteen bishops in a single day. Palladius, who was there, answered that the number was six, and that the case had taken not a day but two years.</p>

<p>While John was away, Constantinople had fallen in love with another preacher. Two visiting bishops had been preaching in the capital. One, Antiochus of Ptolemais, had so fine a voice and delivery, Sozomen says, that some people called him Chrysostom, the golden-mouthed. He made a good deal of money and went home. The other, Severian of Gabala, had written a stock of sermons for the occasion, and he stayed. Serapion sent John reports that Severian was troubling the Church, and Socrates, who did not flatter John, says that the archbishop grew jealous.</p>

<p>Serapion made it worse. When Severian walked past him one day, the deacon stayed in his seat instead of rising for a bishop, and Severian lost his temper. "If Serapion should die a Christian," he shouted, "Christ has not become incarnate." Severian would one day sit among John's judges.</p>

<h2 id="an-empress-and-a-sick-child">An Empress and a Sick Child</h2>

<p>The storm that finally broke over John came from Egypt. Theophilus had driven out a priest named Isidore and a group of monks from the Nitrian desert, among them the brothers known as the Long Brothers. The exiles came to Constantinople to appeal. John received them with honor and let them pray with his people, but he held back their Communion until their case had been examined. Olympias took them in. Theophilus, to cover himself, accused the monks of following the teachings of Origen, and he wrote to Epiphanius, a bishop of Cyprus, urging him to condemn Origen.</p>

<p>Epiphanius came himself. He landed at Hebdomos and went to pray in the church there. John went out with all his clergy to meet him. Epiphanius would not stay in John's house. He even ordained a deacon in one of John's own churches.</p>

<p>Then the empress Eudoxia's son fell dangerously ill, and she sent to Epiphanius to pray for him. Epiphanius sent back word that the child would live if she would have nothing to do with "the heretic Dioscorus" and his companions. Eudoxia sent back an answer of her own.</p>

<p>"If it be the will of God to take my son, His will be done. The Lord who gave me my child, can take him back again. You have not power to raise the dead, otherwise your archdeacon would not have died."</p>

<p>The Long Brothers went to see Epiphanius themselves. Had he read any of their books? He had not. "How is it, then," Ammonius asked him, "that you consider us to be heretics, when you have no proof as to what sentiments we may hold?" When Epiphanius came to the Church of the Apostles, John sent him a message: "You do many things contrary to the canons, Epiphanius." If he roused the people, he might find himself in danger. Epiphanius left the church.</p>

<p>Epiphanius soon sailed for home, and it was reported that he told John, "I hope you will not die a bishop." He died at sea on the way to Cyprus. Next came the man who had sent him.</p>

<h2 id="summoned-to-the-oak">Summoned to the Oak</h2>

<p>In 403 Theophilus of Alexandria came to Constantinople. None of the city's clergy went to meet him, and the sailors of the Alexandrian grain ships cheered him instead. He "came off the ship," John wrote afterward, "hurried past the porch of the Church, and went somewhere outside of the city to lodge." He stayed in the Placidian palace, and he gathered a synod of bishops across the water near Chalcedon, at a place called the Oak.</p>

<p>Origen was hardly mentioned there. Theophilus made his peace with the Long Brothers, and the synod turned to John. His own deacon, also named John, brought twenty-nine charges, and a bishop named Isaac brought eighteen more. Some were serious, and many, Socrates says, were ridiculous. John, they said, had sold the marble Nectarius had set aside for a church, and called Epiphanius a fool and a demon. A bath was heated for him alone. He dressed and undressed on his throne, and ate a lozenge; he ate "gluttonously alone, living like a Cyclops"; and he had taught "If thou sin again, repent again." Two priests came forward to testify against him, Arsacius and Atticus, and both would one day sit on his throne.</p>

<p>The men who presided made no secret of how they felt. Among them were Severian of Gabala and Antiochus of Ptolemais, the preacher some had called golden-mouthed. Cyrinus, the bishop of Chalcedon, called John "the impious," "the haughty," "the inexorable." Socrates adds a small human detail: a bishop from Mesopotamia named Maruthas trod on Cyrinus's foot by accident, and the foot was badly hurt.</p>

<p>Meanwhile forty bishops loyal to John sat with him in his own dining room. Into that room came a summons from the synod at the Oak. John sent back word that he did not choose "to recognize his manifest enemies as judges." Let them remove his open enemies, and he would come and answer any charge. As he put it later, "we were not to come before a judge (we would have appeared ten thousand times before a judge!), but before an enemy and a foe." Four times the summons came, and four times he refused. Then Paul of Heraclea, the bishop John had once asked to make peace in the Ephesus affair, called for the vote. The bishops deposed John for one thing only: refusing to come.</p>

<p>The decree of exile followed. "Late one evening," John remembered, "when I was being escorted through the streets by the whole of the populace, I was arrested by the city governor's agent in the middle of the city, dragged away by force, and put on board a ship, which set sail by night."</p>

<h3 id="torches-on-the-water">Torches on the Water</h3>

<p>He did not stay away long. Severian preached that John deserved his fate for his pride alone, and the people ran howling to the palace. One night, Theodoret writes, there was a great earthquake, and the empress was struck with terror. Palladius says only that "a catastrophe occurred in the royal palace." Whatever it was, Eudoxia sent her eunuch Briso to bring John back from Bithynia, with a message that she had taken no part in the plot against him and had always respected him "as a priest and the initiator of her children." He had baptized them.</p>

<p>When it was known that he was coming, the people put out onto the water and covered the sea with boats and torches. John stopped outside the city and refused to enter until a larger synod had judged his case. The crowd would not wait. They came out singing psalms, many carrying lighted tapers, brought him into his church, and made him sit on his throne and give them the bishop's greeting of peace. He spoke without notes. Theophilus, he said, had plotted against this Church as the king of Egypt once plotted against Sarah, Abraham's wife. Then the man who had told the people of Antioch that he wanted no applause praised the emperor and the empress, and the cheering was so loud that he had to leave his speech half ended.</p>

<p>Theophilus stayed until his Alexandrians and the people of the capital came to blows in the streets. Then, John wrote, he left "like a runaway slave": "at dead of night he secretly flung himself into a boat." Soon afterward sixty bishops met in Constantinople and annulled everything the Oak had done. For a few weeks it looked like victory. It was the last one John would have in that city.</p>

<h2 id="again-herodias-raves">Again Herodias Raves</h2>

<p>Near the great church, on a column of porphyry set on a high platform, a silver statue of the empress was raised and celebrated with applause, dances, and mimes. John said the noise dishonored the Church. Two historians report that the quarrel with Eudoxia flared again, and that John began a sermon with these words: "Again Herodias raves; again she is troubled; she dances again; and again desires to receive John's head in a charger."</p>

<p>When Christmas came, the emperor sent word that he would not receive Communion with John until John had cleared himself. John answered that he was ready to prove his innocence, and his accusers, Sozomen says, did not dare to press their charges. So a new synod found another way. It condemned him for taking back his see after he had been deposed, under a canon from a council held at Antioch by Arians long before. That the canon came from heretics did not matter. It served.</p>

<p>Lent came, and the court ordered John to leave his church. He sent back an answer that his friend Palladius preserved: "I have received this Church," he said, "from God our Saviour, for the care of the salvation of the people, and I cannot desert it; if, then, this is your will—for the city is yours—thrust me out by force."</p>

<p>Easter was coming, and with it the night when the catechumens of the city were to be baptized. The bishops of John's party went to the emperor and the empress at the shrines of the martyrs and begged them to spare the Church on that night. One of them, Paul of Crateia, spoke straight to the empress: "Eudoxia, fear God, and pity your own children; do not dishonour the festival of Christ by the shedding of blood."</p>

<p>She did not listen. On Holy Saturday, as evening was closing in and the baptisms were about to begin, the soldiers came. Palladius names their officer, Lucius, and says he brought four hundred Thracian troops. Priests and deacons were beaten and thrown out still in their vestments, Sozomen writes, while the women wept and the children screamed. He would not describe the rest, in case his book fell into the hands of the unbaptized. John described it himself, in a letter to the bishop of Rome.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>"Women who were in the houses of prayer, unrobed in readiness for baptism on that day, fled naked in face of this savage attack, not even allowed to clothe themselves as womanly decency requires. Many of these were even thrown outside injured, and the fonts were filled with blood, and the holy water dyed red from their wounds."</p>
  <p><cite>— John Chrysostom to Pope Innocent I, 404, in Palladius, Dialogue, chapter 2</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>In the confusion, he wrote, the consecrated wine of the Eucharist was spilled on the women's clothing. "It was exactly like a barbarian man-hunting raid." Then came the morning of Easter. "When day came, the whole city moved outside the walls, and kept the feast under trees and thickets, like sheep scattered abroad." That day the emperor, at a place called Pempton, caught sight of about three thousand of the newly baptized, still in their white robes.</p>

<p>After Easter the danger became personal. A man carrying a dagger was seized by the people as a hired assassin and dragged to the prefect; John sent bishops to free him before he could be tortured. Then a slave of the priest Elpidius ran toward the bishop's house with a dagger and stabbed the people who tried to stop him, and one of them died. Paul of Crateia's plea to the empress for the sake of her children would have an answer, but John would not live to see it.</p>

<h2 id="the-fire-and-the-little-boat">The Fire and the Little Boat</h2>

<p>John put his case to the West. Four bishops and two deacons carried his long letter to Innocent, the bishop of Rome. In it he asked for a just trial. "Scythians and Sarmatians," he wrote, "would never have decided a case after hearing one side only." If what had been done to him were allowed to stand, it would be the end of order among bishops: "Every one will expel his neighbour, and be expelled in turn."</p>

<p>In June 404 the emperor's order came. John obeyed it. He slipped away without the knowledge of the people guarding him, protesting only that he was treated more severely than "murderers, sorcerers, and adulterers," since he had never been tried. A little boat took him across to Bithynia with two companions, Cyriacus and Eulysius. Behind him his enemies had the church doors shut, and the crowd inside smashed them open with stones. In the middle of the struggle, on 20 June, the great church caught fire. The flames spread to the senate house beside it and burned from late afternoon until morning. Each side accused the other of setting it, and no one ever proved who did.</p>

<p>In John's place the court installed Arsacius, Nectarius's brother, the archpriest who had testified against him at the Oak. Palladius, never fair to John's enemies, wrote that he had "less power of speech than a fish, and of action than a frog." Those who would not take Communion with Arsacius paid for it. Deaconesses of high rank were told to accept him or pay two hundred pounds of gold. Olympias was brought before the prefect and charged with setting the fire. "My past life ought to avert all suspicion from me," she said, "for I have devoted my large property to the restoration of the temples of God." When the prefect claimed to know all about her life, she answered that in that case he should step down from the bench and act as her accuser, and let someone else judge. Then she told him plainly: "I will not hold communion with those from whom I ought to secede, nor consent to anything that is not lawful to the pious." He fined her heavily, and she left the city for Cyzicus.</p>

<p>Others suffered worse. A reader named Eutropius died under torture. The ascetic Stephanus was held for ten months for carrying letters from Rome, and the skin was torn from his ribs; "I myself have seen the marks," Palladius wrote. John's friends were scattered to the edges of the empire, Cyriacus to Palmyra and Palladius himself to Syene, near the land of the Blemmyes.</p>

<p>Rome took John's side. Innocent wrote to the clergy of Constantinople: "No accusation was brought, none was heard." He would not break communion with John without a lawful judgment. To John himself he wrote that "God always tries the best of men to see whether they will continue in the height of patience." When Innocent and the Western emperor Honorius sent bishops east with letters asking for a new council, the court of Constantinople sent them home in disgrace, "as if they had invaded a foreign government."</p>

<p>On 30 September a violent hailstorm fell on the city, and four days later the empress Eudoxia died. Many in Constantinople took it as the judgment of God. They said the same when Cyrinus of Chalcedon, the man whose foot had been trodden on, lost both his feet. Socrates, who recorded the talk, declined to judge any of it. "God only knows," he wrote.</p>

<p>John, meanwhile, was on the road east, to a place the court had chosen with care. Palladius believed it had been chosen "in the hope of getting him killed by the Isaurians."</p>

<h2 id="a-mule-fell-in-the-dark">A Mule Fell in the Dark</h2>

<p>The road east ran through Cappadocia. John reached Caesarea late one evening "in the very height of a burning fever," as he told Olympias afterward, "well nigh a dead man." He lay at an inn at the edge of the town, and at first Caesarea could not do enough for him. The clergy and the people came to see him, and monks and nuns, and physicians to treat him. Officials and former governors called every day and "treated me as the apple of their eye." That, he suspected, was the trouble. The bishop of Caesarea, Pharetrius, did not like it, though John was careful to add, "I do not positively declare it but only suspect it to be the fact."</p>

<p>Then news came that Isaurian raiders "in countless multitudes" were overrunning the district and had burned a large village. The soldiers marched out, and even the old men went up to defend the walls. At dawn "a rabble of monks (for so I must call them, indicating their frenzy by the expression)" rushed the house where the sick man lay and threatened to set it on fire. The imperial soldiers ran to him for protection. "Even if we are to fall into the hands of the Isaurians," they begged, "deliver us from these wild beasts." The governor came and could do nothing. The local priests hid, "covered with shame and blushes," because they said the monks were acting on Pharetrius's orders. At high noon John had himself carried out in a litter, while the crowd shrieked and cursed their bishop. One friend put it bluntly: "Depart I entreat you; fall into the hands of the Isaurians, provided you get clear away from us."</p>

<p>A lady named Seleucia took him to her villa, five miles out, and told her steward to arm the farmhands if the monks came. But Pharetrius pressed her too, and at midnight the priest Evethius woke John. "Get up, I pray you, the barbarians are upon us, they are close at hand." Out they went. "It was midnight," John wrote, "a dark, murky night without a moon." The priest had the torches put out "lest the barbarians should be attracted by the light." On the rough and stony road "the mule which carried my litter fell on its knees," and John was thrown out and nearly killed. He went on dragged along on foot by Evethius, "led, or rather hauled by the hand, for to walk was impossible." Only later did he learn that the alarm had been raised by Seleucia herself, who was ashamed to admit the pressure Pharetrius had put on her.</p>

<p>The young man who wrote <em>On the Priesthood</em> had feared the wild beasts of his own soul. At Caesarea the wild beasts had turned out to be monks. Do you not think, he asked Olympias, that these sufferings alone "would avail to blot out many of my sins"? Then he told her how she should take the news.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>"Wherefore also I beseech your Honour to rejoice at these things, to be glad, and leap for joy, and to glorify God who has counted me worthy to suffer such things."</p>
  <p><cite>— John Chrysostom, Letter to Olympias, written after Caesarea</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<h3 id="letters-from-a-lonely-town">Letters from a Lonely Town</h3>

<p>The place of exile was Cucusus, on the border of Cilicia and Armenia, "a petty and lonely town," Theodoret called it. From there, and from the roads on either side of it, came nearly all of the 242 letters of John that survive, seventeen of them to Olympias. She was sunk in despondency after the persecution, and from far away he set out to treat her grief like a wound.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>"Why do you lament? why do you belabour yourself, and demand of yourself a punishment which your enemies were not able to demand from you, having thus abandoned your soul to the tyranny of dejection?"</p>
  <p><cite>— John Chrysostom, Letter to Olympias, from Cucusus</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>"Is it the desolation of this place which grieves you?" he asked. "Yet what can be pleasanter than my sojourn here?" She was not to worry about the winter, or his digestion, or the Isaurians. He kept a constant fire, set screens around his room, used "a large number of rugs," and stayed always indoors. When he fell ill, a remedy sent by a friend named Syncletion cured him in three days, and he asked for more. Then he turned the tables on his patroness. If she would take the same care of herself, she would be far better. "Do not then now desire death, nor neglect the means of cure."</p>

<p>Another letter tells what the winter had really done. For two months, he admitted, "I have been no better than one dead, nay worse." Shut in one smoky room, he had suffered "perpetual vomiting supervening on headache, loss of appetite, and constant sleeplessness," until "day and dawn and noon were all one night to me." With the spring, all his troubles vanished. He had waited to tell her until the worst was over.</p>

<p>He did not pretend that suffering was pleasant. "Sin is the only thing which is really distressing," he wrote; "all other things are but dust and smoke." Prison, chains, exile, the loss of goods were "mere words." And to a friend in grief he wrote of affliction in the language of the goldsmith.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>"And as the fire makes the piece of gold, when it is applied to it, of better proof: so also affliction when it visits golden characters renders them purer and more proven."</p>
  <p><cite>— John Chrysostom, Letter to Olympias, from exile</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>He sent her a treatise whose title was its whole argument: <em>No One Can Harm the Man Who Does Not Injure Himself</em>. Synods could depose him, empresses exile him, monks threaten to burn the house over his head. None of it could touch his soul unless he let it. The fear of his youth had been that the bishop's throne would feed the beasts that tear souls apart. Now the throne was gone, and the soul, as far as anyone could see, was whole.</p>

<p>Exile did not make him idle. With money from Olympias and others he bought back captives from the Isaurian robbers and sent them home to their families, and visitors came from Antioch and Cilicia to see him. When famine struck, Palladius says, he fed the poor "not with corn, so much as with words." He still cared for the Church among the Goths. Word came that Unilas, "that excellent bishop whom I lately ordained and sent into Gothia," had died, and John wrote to keep his enemies from choosing the next one. To friends who complained that his letters were short, he answered: "I am not surprised that you call my long letter a short one. For this is just the way with lovers."</p>

<p>But his enemies had not finished with him. They moved him to Arabissus, and then a new order came: he was to be taken to Pityus, on the Black Sea.</p>

<h2 id="tomorrow-we-shall-be-together">Tomorrow We Shall Be Together</h2>

<p>The soldiers who took him toward Pityus hurried him on, and for three months they marched him at forced speed. One of the soldiers was kind to him. Another was savage, and Palladius says he preferred to march the old man in heavy rain and in the full heat of the sun. John's body, broken long ago in the cave, began to fail.</p>

<p>They came to Comana in Pontus and passed through the town without stopping. Five or six miles beyond it they lodged at the shrine of the martyr Basiliscus. That night, Palladius writes, the martyr stood beside John and spoke to him: "Be of good cheer, brother; to-morrow we shall be together."</p>

<p>In the morning John begged his guards to let him stay where he was until eleven o'clock. They refused and pushed on. They had gone about thirty furlongs when he was taken so ill that they had to bring him back to the shrine.</p>

<p>There he asked for white clothes. He took off everything he was wearing and changed it all, down to his shoes, and what he had taken off he gave away to those who stood around him. Then he received Communion, and he prayed his last prayer in the words he always used.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>"Glory to God for all things."</p>
  <p><cite>— John Chrysostom's last words, in Palladius, Dialogue on the Life of St. John Chrysostom, chapter 11</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>He died that day, about sixty years old. Socrates gives the date as 14 September 407. He was buried in the martyr's shrine, beside Basiliscus, "for so the martyr had ordained in a dream," Theodoret says. Crowds came, Palladius writes, from Syria, from Cilicia, from Pontus, and from Armenia.</p>

<p>The prisoner had died in white, the color the newly baptized had worn on the morning after the soldiers broke up their Easter. He had nothing left to give away but his clothes, and he gave them. In Antioch, in the season of danger, he had kept blessing God. At the end of the road he did the same. The young man who had been so afraid that a bishop's honors would devour his soul had lost every honor a bishop could have. He had not lost his soul, and he had not stopped giving thanks.</p>

<p>In Constantinople, the men who had driven him out could now forget him. The Church would not let them.</p>

<h2 id="the-emperor-laid-his-head">The Emperor Laid His Head</h2>

<p>Within a year of his death, Palladius was already sure how the story would end. "John will be honoured as a martyr," he wrote, "when those who are set against his good reputation are brought to dust." The bishops of the West refused communion with the Eastern bishops until John's name was restored to the diptychs, the lists of names read aloud at the altar. Alexander, the bishop of Antioch, was the first to restore it, in John's own city, with a procession. Atticus, the priest who had testified against him at the Oak and was now bishop of Constantinople, did the same. Name by name, the Church took him back.</p>

<p>Thirty-one years after his death the Church of Constantinople brought him home. Socrates gives the credit to Proclus, the bishop of the city, who obtained the emperor's permission to move John's body from Comana. Theodoret gives the credit to the emperor Theodosius II. Once again the faithful put out onto the water. Their boats were packed so close, Theodoret says, that they turned the sea into land, and their torches covered the mouth of the Bosphorus. The city had lit the same water to bring John back alive. Now it lit the way for his coffin.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>"After first gazing upon the bier he laid his head against it, and prayed for his parents and for pardon on them who had ignorantly sinned."</p>
  <p><cite>— Theodoret, Ecclesiastical History, Book V, 36</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>The body was carried in solemn procession through the city and laid with great honor in the Church of the Apostles, where Eudoxia had been buried. Socrates gives the day: 27 January, in the sixteenth consulship of the emperor Theodosius, the year 438. Those who had stayed apart from the Church for John's sake came back into communion. Socrates could not resist a last remark. John, he wrote, "was restored to communion by Proclus in the thirty-fifth year after his death!"</p>

<p>Later Orthodox tradition tells more. The Orthodox Life of the translation says that the bearers could not lift the coffin until the emperor wrote to John asking his pardon. It says the body was found incorrupt, that John opened his mouth and said "Peace be to all," and that many of the sick were healed at his tomb. None of this appears in Socrates or Theodoret, who wrote within living memory of the event. It belongs to the later, loving memory of the Church, and it is told here as legend. The fifth-century historians give something plainer and harder to forget. Paul of Crateia had begged Eudoxia to pity her own children. Now the emperor had come to the man the court had driven out, laid his head on his coffin, and prayed for his parents.</p>

<h3 id="golden-mouth-east-and-west">Golden Mouth, East and West</h3>

<p>In John's own lifetime the name "Chrysostom" was given to at least one other preacher: Antiochus of Ptolemais, who sat among John's judges at the Oak. In time it became John's. The Orthodox Church keeps his feast on 13 November, because he died on the feast of the Exaltation of the Cross, and the return of his relics on 27 January. Under the emperor Alexios Komnenos, Christians in Constantinople quarreled over who was the greatest teacher of the Church, Basil the Great, Gregory the Theologian, or John. According to the Orthodox liturgical tradition, the three appeared to John Mauropous, the metropolitan of Euchaita, and told him, "We three are one." He set a single feast for all three on 30 January, the feast of the Three Hierarchs.</p>

<p>In 1204 the armies of the Fourth Crusade sacked Constantinople, and Orthodox accounts say that John's relics were taken to Rome and placed in old St. Peter's Basilica. The Catholic Church honors him as a Doctor of the Church and keeps his feast on 13 September. Until the calendar was changed, the Western feast fell on 27 January, and Mozart was named for it. In November 2004 Pope John Paul II returned a portion of the relics of John and of Gregory the Theologian to the Ecumenical Patriarch Bartholomew, writing of the need to "purify our wounded memories." The patriarch called the return an act that "atones for an anomaly and an ecclesiastical injustice." A skull and an ear venerated as John's are kept at Vatopedi on Mount Athos, and a head is venerated in Moscow.</p>

<p>His name is spoken at the altar in the Divine Liturgy of St. John Chrysostom. He did not compose it as it stands: an eighth-century manuscript ascribes only two of its prayers to him, and the whole Liturgy first appears under his name in the eleventh century. The Paschal homily read under his name at Pascha is likewise not his; the earliest attribution to him comes from Theodore of Stoudios in the ninth century. But the Church that prays these words in his name prays them in the spirit of a man who wrote this about the altar.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>"At such a time angels stand by the Priest; and the whole sanctuary, and the space round about the altar, is filled with the powers of heaven, in honor of Him who lies thereon."</p>
  <p><cite>— John Chrysostom, On the Priesthood, Book VI, 4</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>He had seen two altars all his life: the one where the angels stand, and the one lying in the lanes and the market places, made of the poor. He served both, and he spoke as plainly to emperors as he did to beggars. It cost him his see, his city, and in the end his life. As a young man he had hidden from the bishop's throne because he feared it would devour his soul. The throne was taken from him, and he died a prisoner in a borrowed shrine, in white, with nothing left to give away. His last words were the ones he had always used: "Glory to God for all things."</p>

<h2 id="a-note-on-the-sources">A Note on the Sources</h2>

<p>The oldest witnesses do not always agree. Palladius, John's friend, wrote within a year or two of his death and is openly partisan. John's letter to Innocent survives only in his <em>Dialogue</em>, and Innocent's replies only in Sozomen. Socrates says Evagrius ordained John a priest; Palladius says Flavian. Socrates and Sozomen place the first exile at Praenetus, Theodoret at Hieron, and they say John gave himself up at noon, unseen, where his own letter describes an arrest in the street at evening. The accounts of Easter 404 differ on whether the raid fell on a church or on public baths, and name the baths for three different emperors. Theophilus said John deposed sixteen bishops in Asia, Palladius says six, and Sozomen thirteen. Photius, who read the acts of the Oak, lists twenty-nine charges by the deacon John and eighteen by Isaac and counts forty-five voters; Palladius cites minutes with thirty-six names. For 438 Socrates credits Proclus, Theodoret the emperor. Libanius's deathbed remark is reported only by Sozomen. The "Herodias" words are reported only by Socrates and Sozomen, and the sermon that survives under that opening is judged spurious. No ancient source gives the year of his birth or of his baptism, and who first called John "Chrysostom" is unresolved. "The road to hell is paved with the skulls of bishops" is a later embellishment of his line on Acts quoted above, and no source was found in his works for "the hottest places in hell" or "the Church is a hospital."</p>

<h2 id="how-this-was-researched">How This Life Was Researched</h2>

<p>This life was written from thirty-two sources in English and Greek, gathered in four research passes: John's own words, the early witnesses, the Church's later memory and miracles, and a second look at his last years. Its core is four fifth-century accounts, the <em>Dialogue</em> of his friend Palladius and the church histories of Socrates, Sozomen, and Theodoret, with Photius's ninth-century summary of the acts of the Oak, beside John's own sermons, treatise, and letters in the public-domain Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers translations. The sermons against the Jews are described from secondary sources only, because the one modern English translation is under copyright. Not read were the orations of Libanius, Pope Vigilius's document of 553, the Greek of Socrates and Theodoret, the rest of Theodoret's fifth book, Sozomen's chapters on the riot at Antioch and on Severian, Baring-Gould, and Butler in its own edition.</p>

